=RText Users Guide

== The .rtext config file

A .rtext config file is used to specify which backend service is to be started for a given
RText model file.

In order to be found by an RText plugin, the .rtext file must be located in the same directory
as the RText model file being edited or in any of its parent directories. The frontend uses the
first .rtext file which holds an entry matching the model file; a .rtext file without a matching
entry does not stop the search.

Here is the grammar for the config file syntax:

  rtext_config      ::= <service_spec>+
  service_spec      ::= <file_pattern_list>:\n<command line string>\n
  file_pattern_list ::= <file_pattern> | <file_pattern>, <file_pattern_list>
  file_pattern      ::= <filename without extension> | *.<extension>

Lines which are part of no service spec are ignored, so blank lines and comments may be placed
between specs.

Patterns are matched by name, not by globbing. A model file which has an extension is looked up
as "*.<extension>", so it is matched by that pattern and by no other: "*.ext1" does not match a
file named "a.ext11", and a pattern spelled out as "model.ext1" matches nothing. A model file
which has no extension is looked up as its filename, so it is matched by a pattern equal to that
filename.

Here is an example:

  *.ext1:
  ruby service1.rb 
  *.ext2, *.ext3:
  ruby service2.rb *.ext2 *.ext3
  noext:
  service3.sh

This example contains specifications for three different backend services. service1.rb is started
when a model file with the extension "ext1" is being edited. service2.rb is started when a 
model file with extension "ext2" or "ext3" is edited. service2.rb also receives additional command
line parameters. service3.sh is started whenever a file named "noext" is edited.

The command line is run with the working directory set to the directory holding the .rtext file,
so relative paths within it are relative to that directory. The frontend redirects the command's
standard output and standard error to a file, from which it reads the port the backend listens on
(see the "Transport" section of the RText protocol documentation).

== The backend service

RText::Service is the backend service implementation. It answers RText protocol requests until
nothing has asked anything for an idle timeout, which defaults to 60 seconds and is set with the
:timeout option.

While it runs, the service holds two files in the working directory:

* .rtext.lock -- held for as long as the service runs. A second service started in the same
  directory waits one second for the lock and then fails, so only one service serves a directory.
* .rtext.config -- YAML holding the "port" the service listens on and its "pid". It is removed
  when the service terminates.

Both paths default to the working directory and are set with the :lock_file_path and
:config_file_path options.
